%N A399980 Triangle T(n,k) (1<=k<=n) read by rows: T(n,k) = number of distinct k-inversion sets in the symmetric group on n elements.
%C A399980 A k-inversion in a permutation w is a set of k distinct values appearing in decreasing order in the one-line notation for w.
%H A399980 Sara Billey, Herman Chau, and Kevin Liu. <a href="http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31795">Affine Higher Bruhat Orders</a>, arXiv:2610.31795 [math.CO], Oct 2026.
%F A399980 By definition, T(1,n)=1, T(2,n)=n!, T(n,n)=2.
%e A399980 The 2-inversions of w are the usual inversions on pairs of values appearing in decreasing order in the one-line notation for w.  Since each permutation has a unique inversion set, T(2,n)=n!.
%e A399980 The 3-inversions of w=[4,3,1,2] are the triples appearing in reverse order, so [1,3,4] and [2,3,4]. For k=3, n=4, the T(3,4)=9 possible 3-inversion sets among all permutations in S_4 are {}, {[1, 2, 4]}, {[2, 3, 4]}, {[1, 2, 3]}, {[1, 3, 4]}, {[1, 3, 4], [2, 3, 4]}, {[1, 2, 4], [1, 3, 4]}, {[1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 4]}, {[1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 4], [1, 3, 4], [2, 3, 4]}.
%e A399980 The triangle begins:
%e A399980    1;
%e A399980    1,    2;
%e A399980    1,    6,    2;
%e A399980    1,   24,    9,   2;
